WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Plans for a new starship static fire site in Florida, move the upper stage catch test campaign

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:13.080
closer to dual coast operations. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 69. Starship Flight 14 could

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.180
launch as early as late August and would mark the first attempt to catch the ship upper stage

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:24.460
with the launch tower arms. The test follows earlier booster catch demonstrations and targets a net

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:30.360
timeline of late August. Success would validate a new recovery path for the upper stage hardware

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:37.040
that has so far relied on ocean splashdown. Engineers are monitoring propellant loads and structural

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.940
loads on the ship's catch points during the descent burn sequence. The ship upper stage would execute

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.940
the final hover and positioning sequence using only its reaction control thrusters and main engine

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.420
gimbal after the payload bay is empty. Remember, we covered the starship program yesterday.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.620
This catch attempt moves the reuse goals forward by testing upper stage recovery for the first time.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.060
Project ignition would place a dedicated static fire facility at Kennedy Space Center for starship

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.700
upper stage engines. The site would allow hot fire testing without relying solely on the Texas

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:18.420
starbase infrastructure. Permit documents outline concrete foundations, flame trenches,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.440
and propellant storage sized for repeated raptor firings. This addition supports higher flight

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:30.300
cadence by decoupling test campaigns from launch pad availability. The Florida stand-on would enable

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.920
separate test campaigns on the ship while Texas infrastructure remains focused on integrated launch

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.400
and catch operations. Additional filings expand on earlier permit requests for a

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.540
dedicated test stand at the Space Coast and target static fire operations for both booster and ship

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.180
elements. Local environmental reviews are underway to assess noise and exhaust dispersion

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:59.560
and the filing specify flame deflector geometry and deluge water systems sized for the full raptor

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:06.400
cluster. Two Falcon 9 missions are now targeted for mid-August from opposite coasts. The global star

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:15.600
2R mission carrying nine satellites is scheduled for August 16, UTC from SLC 40 with booster B1090 on

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:24.300
its 14th flight landing at LZ 40 after a 41-day turnaround. The US SF-366 National Security

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:34.180
Payload is scheduled for August 15, UTC from Vandenberg, SLC4E with booster B1088 on its 18th flight,

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:41.840
targeting the drone ship OCIS-LY after a 48-day turnaround. Weather constraints and range

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.360
availability remain the final variables for the Florida return to pad adjacent landing zone 40.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:53.320
The Space Force awarded five companies 60 million dollars for multi-vendor satellite network

00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:01.160
tests. Amazon LEO Lockheed Martin, Northrop Grumman, Rocket Lab, and York Space received contracts

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:07.300
to prototype links to the SpaceX-built data network backbone. The work tests interoperability

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:13.060
across commercial and government satellite constellations through the existing Starsheel-derived relay

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:19.120
architecture. Bulgaria approved a draft memorandum with Starbase Europe to develop a new space

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.980
and defense innovation hub. The collaboration focuses on technology development and supply chain

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.380
capabilities to support component testing and integration work for European launch and satellite

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:37.100
programs. Observers on local Florida forums are tracking Sonic Boom modeling for the upcoming Falcon 9

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:43.280
return to LZ40 and comparing predicted over-pressure levels with prior missions from the same pad.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.180
Reddit users noted the short 4-day pad turnaround claimed for the upcoming Global Star mission

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:53.940
and questioned whether it reflects a new operational baseline for SLC40.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:04:02.200
Community trackers highlighted the 18-flight count on booster B1088 for the US SF-366 mission

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.940
and discussed remaining life limits on the Falcon 9 fleet after repeated high inclination flights.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:14.340
Worth keeping an eye on, SpaceX, AI, and Dharana hybrid are locked in counter lawsuits over

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:21.060
as much as $570 million in disputed payments. The filing center on contract performance and

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:26.770
termination clauses with resolution requiring either settlement or a court ruling on the underlying

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:32.770
obligations. The dispute involves competing claims on milestone deliverables and payment schedules

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:39.330
tied to prior engineering work. On the AI front, SpaceX's NVIDIA Alliance is being positioned to

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:45.650
extend from terrestrial GPU clusters into orbital and deep space applications according to investor

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:51.910
commentary. The partnership would leverage existing GPU supply agreements to support both ground-based

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:59.070
training clusters and potential on-orbit inference nodes. Musk confirmed the 4th SpaceX AI data center

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:04.470
in Memphis while noting that illegal gas turbines at the site are being removed as part of the

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:11.930
broader Colossus class build-out supporting XAI training runs. GROC 4.6 has launched, leaving one

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:17.850
remaining XAI Odyssey project before the stated ultimate AI goal is reached, and the release

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:22.670
adds new inference optimizations that reduce latency for developer-facing tools.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.750
Cursor agents now launch three times faster following recent optimizations that improve agent

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.870
handoff speed inside the editor environment used by developers integrating GROC models.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:40.270
From an engineering standpoint, the planned tower catch of the ship upper stage on Starship Flight 14

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:45.790
introduces a new set of first principles constraints compared with the super-heavy booster catch

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:51.430
already demonstrated. The ship descends under a different mass distribution and with a different

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:57.130
center of gravity location once the payload bay is empty, so the vehicle must execute a precise

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:03.370
flip and hover before the tower arms close. The mechanical interface must absorb both the residual

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:09.290
velocity from the final landing burn and any lateral drift caused by wind or residual propellant

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:15.110
slosh. Because the ship lacks the booster's grid fin control surfaces, the reaction control

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:20.530
thrusters and main engine gambling become the only means of fine positioning in the final seconds.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:26.690
The catch arms must therefore tolerate higher lateral loads than those seen during booster recovery

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:31.070
while still preserving the ship's forward structural integrity for potential reuse.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.610
If the catch succeeds, it removes the need for a separate downrange recovery fleet

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:42.110
and the associated ocean splash-down hardware, collapsing the recovery cost toward the raw

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:47.930
propellant and maintenance expense of the engines themselves. That reduction is the same logic that

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:53.750
made booster reuse economical. The idiot index for the ship drops when the expensive heat shield

00:06:53.750 --> 00:07:00.030
tiles and avionics no longer risk loss on every flight. The open engineering question is how many

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:05.410
additional structural reinforcements the ship's forward section will require to survive the arm

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:11.490
loads without adding prohibitive dry mass. Each kilogram of added reinforcement directly reduces

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:17.170
payload capacity to orbit, so designers face a classic mass fraction trade-off between catch

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:23.850
reliability and mission performance. Florida's new static fire site under project ignition directly

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:29.950
supports this path by allowing repeated raptor firings on the ship without tying up the Texas launch

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:36.210
mount. Each test cycle burns through a known quantity of methane and oxygen. The cost floor is

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:41.990
therefore set by the propellant price and the labor hours to reload the test stand. Faster iteration

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:48.030
on the ship's landing burn profile becomes possible once the Florida stand is online, shortening

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:54.330
the timeline to a reliable tower catch sequence. The stand's flame trench and deluge system are sized

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:59.650
for the full nine engine cluster, enabling full duration burns that replicate the thermal and

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:05.670
acoustic environment of an actual descent. This separation of test and launch infrastructure mirrors

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:10.870
the approach used in earlier Falcon programs where dedicated test stands accelerated engine

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:17.810
qualification without constraining flight rate. A quick look at the markets shows SNPCX closed that

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:26.810
$141.29. Before we go, Starship Flight 14's upper-stage catch attempt remains the clearest near-term

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:32.710
test of whether the program's reuse architecture can scale. That covers everything worth knowing about

